We would first like to sincerely thank you for the hard work and extra efforts required to manage the ICLR 2026 review process under these unusual circumstances. We greatly appreciate your time, care, and dedication in ensuring a fair and rigorous evaluation for all submissions. To better assist your review and decision-making process, we provide below a brief summary of our rebuttal and the corresponding manuscript updates for your consideration.

We were encouraged that multiple reviewers independently recognised several key strengths of our work: (1) the significance and novelty of our proposed task (Reviewers h9UB, 5Q3B, amxD, LGBp), (2) the solidity and scale of our experimental design and evaluation (Reviewers h9UB, 5Q3B, LGBp), and (3) the theoretical grounding of our agentic framework (Reviewers LGBp, h9UB).

In the rebuttal, we carefully addressed each reviewer’s concern and incorporated corresponding revisions into the manuscript (marked in blue). We summarise the main points below.

**On novelty and technical contribution**, we clarified that our contribution is not a superficial composition of existing tools, but a principled agentic workflow grounded in economic theory. In particular, we emphasised the attribute-to-feature mapping inspired by Bayesian persuasion as a novel mechanism enabling value-relevant translation from structured data—beyond what standard prompting or RAG alone can achieve. We also clarified the rigor and role of our human-expert baselines as a proper industry-level benchmark.

**On evaluation scope and generalisability**, we clarified that this work is intended as a foundational, methodologically rigorous benchmark, with a deep human-subject evaluation in a single complex market to establish internal validity. We further clarified our strict definition of factuality based solely on structured listing attributes to ensure reproducibility and fair comparison between humans and models, with unsupported claims penalised as hallucinations.

We also made **concrete technical updates**, including:

* Adding bootstrapped confidence intervals to the ELO analysis for statistical robustness;

* Explicitly acknowledging cross-market and cross-cultural limitations;

* Clarifying the RAG retrieval protocol;

* Expanding the justification and internal diversity analysis of the Chicago testbed (entropy analysis and qualitative robustness studies);

While we did not receive many post-rebuttal follow-up comments from reviewers before the discussion phase was cut short, we believe their technical concerns were directly and fully addressed through both our written responses and the corresponding manuscript updates. We therefore hope that our rebuttal and revisions can be fully taken into account when estimating how reviewer impressions might have evolved with additional discussion time.

We sincerely thank the Area Chair again for the careful and thoughtful consideration of our work.
